834	    /**
835	     * Rejects creating an owner whose identity duplicates an existing owner's. All duplicate detection
836	     * is consolidated onto the single derived {@code identityKey} (see
837	     * {@link Households#identityKey(String, String, String)}): the SHA-256 hex over the normalized
838	     * telephone, the lower-cased email (a {@code null} email compared as an empty segment) and the
839	     * {@code soundex} of the last name. Two owners are duplicates when their identity keys are equal.
840	     * Existing telephones are re-normalized to E.164 before comparison, so equivalent values submitted
841	     * in different formats (e.g. national {@code "0412 345 678"} and international
842	     * {@code "+61 412 345 678"}) still match. The email-domain blocklist is applied first (during
843	     * email normalization), and soft-deleted owners are ignored.
844	     * <p>
845	     * Because the telephone is part of the key, two members of one household (same last name and
846	     * postcode) with different telephones have different identity keys and are both allowed; such a
847	     * near-match is instead surfaced as a soft duplicate (see {@link #findPossibleDuplicateOf}).
848	     *
849	     * @param owner the owner being created, with its telephone and email already normalized
850	     * @throws DuplicateIdentityException (409 Conflict) if another owner has the same identity key
851	     */
852	    private void rejectDuplicateIdentity(Owner owner) {
853	        String identity = Households.identityKey(owner.getTelephone(), owner.getEmail(), owner.getLastName());
854	        boolean duplicate = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
855	            .filter(this::isNotDeleted)
856	            .anyMatch(existing -> identity.equals(Households.identityKey(
857	                toE164OrNull(existing.getTelephone()), existing.getEmail(), existing.getLastName())));
858	        if (duplicate) {
859	            throw new DuplicateIdentityException(identity);
860	        }
861	    }
